A1. A kind of jam found in all large cities. ~ TRAFFIC
A6. Chicago's population consists largely of these. ~ BANDITS
A12. What most boys learn at college. ~ RAH
A13. This president knew enough about wild animals to cope with Congress (abbr.) ~ TR
A14. To accomplish. ~ DO
A15. What Scotchmen wear over the weak end. ~ TAM
A16. Pronoun—first person—Irish. ~ OI
A17. This conjunction allows an alternative. ~ OR
A19. A loud speaker heard in most homes. ~ MA
A20. Lucky number in a crap game in Ancient Rome. ~ XI
A21. You might use this to get up in the world. ~ TNT
A23. Feminine styles are showing more and more of this. ~ ANATOMY
A26. This doesn't amount to anything. ~ NIL
A27. Possessive pronoun. ~ ITS
A29. What Annie Laurie would call vertical 24. ~ TWA
A30. The one secret she will keep. ~ AGE
A32. A shocking little thing. ~ ION
A34. One place where autoes will never displace horses. ~ RODEO
A37. These people make very close friends. ~ SCOTS
A42. Horizontal 6 does this. ~ WAYLAY
A43. Many brave men have lost their freedom here. ~ ALTARS
A44. These are the berries for wine. ~ ELDER
A46. The mug you love to kiss. ~ STEIN
A47. When movie stars marry (contraction). ~ OFT
A50. Water impregnated with alkaline salt imbibed from the ashes of wood. ~ LYE
A52. To injure or impair. ~ MAR
A53. An ex-official of the goat riding club (abbr.). ~ PGM
A56. What the first loud speaker was made from. ~ RIB
A58. These run through the big cities. ~ AVENUES
A61. A certain make of car (not so common as vertical 28). ~ REO
A63. What Elinor Glynn says you need to be a success. ~ IT
A64. The end of Romeo. ~ EO
A65. A couple of these together means "good-bye." ~ TA
A66. What a penny is worth in Alabama (abbr.). ~ CT
A67. You need this to get up in the world. ~ PEP
A69. A very personal pronoun. ~ ME
A70. The bum's highway (abbr.). ~ RR
A72. A pronoun. ~ SHE
A73. A misnomer—these stag affairs should be called "drinkers." ~ SMOKERS
A74. The big stick. ~ ALCOHOL

D1. This will get you in a plight. ~ TROTH
D2. According to the Law of Nature this is wet. (No Congress can repeal this law.) ~ RAIN
D3. Expression of satisfaction. ~ AH
D4. What chorus girls raise instead of children (abbr.). ~ FT
D5. These kind of men should be kept in a safe place. ~ IRON
D7. This man fell early for a woman's applesauce. ~ ADAM
D8. This has prevented many unhappy marriages. ~ NO
D9. A pronoun. ~ IT
D10. These either hold you up or knock you down. ~ TAXI
D11. Efficacious argument used by the pretty girl driver with the traffic cop. ~ SMILE
